WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:05.500
Last clip you connected your tools.
Now your agent learns to use them,

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.333
because it can know GTM OS
before you type a single prompt.

00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:12.033
That's what skills are for.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.967
By the end you'll know the four groups
of skills and how an agent picks one.

00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:21.467
You'll also keep them
fresh and add your own.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.467
Without skills, Mia explains the
scoring model to Orbit every morning,

00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:29.933
and Orbit guesses how HubSpot
behaves inside GTM OS.

00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:33.900
With them, Orbit starts
the day already knowing.

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.633
A skill is a packaged instruction set

00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:40.033
an agent loads when
your request matches it.

00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:44.700
When you ran gtm init, it placed
them in your project: in

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.933
.claude/skills for Claude Code,
and in .codex/skills and

00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:50.833
.agents/skills for Codex.

00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:55.233
They come from a published
skills release, checked by hash,

00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:58.000
with an offline copy bundled in the CLI.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.533
So init works without a network, and
it never trusts a file it can't verify.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:06.667
First, the groups. There's a
large set, so think in four.

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:10.933
Setup and onboarding: the skills behind
/setup-gtmos and the setup wizard.

00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:15.767
Per engine: scoring and lead work,
signals, outreach, research, SEO and GEO,

00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:16.633
ads and content.

00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:21.067
Tool skills: HubSpot, Salesforce,
Composio, Search Console, GA4, Gmail,

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:25.067
Slack and more. Each tells the agent
how that tool behaves inside GTM OS.

00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:29.067
And looot skills, for enrichment,
verification and search data.

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:32.433
One more sits across all
four: the engine catalog,

00:01:32.433 --> 00:01:36.200
which explains every engine's
purpose, connections and settings.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.400
Beside the skills sit role subagents,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.600
like the revenue operations
analyst and the deal reviewer,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.300
that you call by name.

00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:47.633
Second, how an agent picks one. Each
skill carries a short description.

00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:49.167
The agent reads only those,

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:52.633
and loads the full instructions
when your request matches.

00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:55.300
Ask your agent: "Score my
accounts against our ICP",

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.167
and it loads the scoring skill first.

00:01:58.233 --> 00:02:01.467
Ask for first lines, and it
loads the first lines skill,

00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:05.200
which writes one cited fact
per lead, or says no fact.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.733
You never name the skill. A
skill teaches the agent how.

00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:10.267
Its key still decides what it may do,

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:14.200
so instructions can update freely
while permissions don't move.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:18.033
Third, the slash commands:
shortcuts to the big jobs,

00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:20.900
typed in Claude Code
in the project folder.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.200
/setup-gtmos installs
and connects from zero.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.533
/build-gtm-system builds
your system end to end.

00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:30.633
The rest cover the daily
work: /create-play,

00:02:30.633 --> 00:02:33.067
/wire-connector and /describe-ops.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.967
The one to remember is /go-live-check,

00:02:35.967 --> 00:02:39.467
a read only go or no go
before you leave Dry Mode.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:41.767
Ask your agent: "/go-live-check".

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:45.633
It answers with a verdict,
and lists what's left.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.633
Fourth, keep them fresh. Skills change
as GTM OS does. In the terminal:

00:02:49.633 --> 00:02:52.000
gtm init with the refresh flag takes new

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.900
skills and updates the
ones you haven't touched.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.000
It keeps the ones you edited,
plus any files of your own.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.233
Then restart Claude Code, because
it reads skills only at startup.

00:03:02.267 --> 00:03:04.633
How do you know when you
need it? Run gtm doctor.

00:03:04.633 --> 00:03:07.533
Its Harness section
compares the version stamps

00:03:07.533 --> 00:03:09.633
from your last init with your CLI,

00:03:09.633 --> 00:03:12.033
the skills release and the live server.

00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:15.767
If they differ, it warns that skills
are stale and tells you to refresh.

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:18.733
Or ask your agent: "Are
my skills up to date".

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:22.000
Fifth, add your own. Make a
folder under .claude/skills.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.033
Add a skill file with a name, a
description and your instructions.

00:03:26.033 --> 00:03:28.900
Copy it to the Codex folders
if your team uses Codex.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.967
Refresh never touches your own files.

00:03:30.967 --> 00:03:34.700
Or ask your agent: "Write me a skill
for how we qualify inbound leads,

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.900
and save it in the project".

00:03:36.967 --> 00:03:41.033
Pro move: a shipped skill you
edit is kept exactly as it is,

00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:42.767
so it stops receiving updates.

00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:47.833
To keep both, copy it under a new
name, and the original keeps updating.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.300
Fair concern: instructions from
a release, running in my agent?

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.933
Each skill is checksum verified
plain text you can read,

00:03:55.933 --> 00:03:57.933
and a skill only instructs.

00:03:57.933 --> 00:04:02.500
What your agent may actually
do is still decided by its key.

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:06.133
Replay: Four groups,
picked by description.

00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:11.733
Refresh when doctor says stale,
and add your own beside them.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:16.033
Your agent now knows the product,
the engines and your tools,

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:18.833
and you can teach it
your own way of working.

00:04:18.833 --> 00:04:23.733
Up next, predefined agents, a team
that works inside the rules you set.
